"The prognosis is influenced by several factors. Positive prognostic indicators include negative surgical margins, absence of seminal vesicle invasion, and no lymph node metastasis reported in the prostatectomy specimen. These findings suggest a favorable local disease course. The preoperative PSA was 4.8 ng/mL, which contributes to the risk assessment. A countervailing finding is the pathological Gleason score of 4+4 (ISUP grade group 4) from the surgical specimen, indicating high-grade cancer. Furthermore, the biopsy showed Gleason 4+4 in the right side with a cribriform pattern, consistent with the higher grade seen post-surgery. The nodal status was assessed and reported as negative for metastasis. Prognosis revision would be significantly aided by postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent trajectory over time. The fixed estimated horizon is 69.39 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 55.52–83.27 months; its coverage has not been validated."
